Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Before I build the board summary and risk list, one quick clarification so the numbers are accurate:

- When you say “20 months runway” and “burn rate $72K/month,” those conflict. At $72K net burn/month, $2.1M cash ≈ 29.2 months of runway (2,100,000 / 72,000 = 29.17). A 20-month runway implies a net cash burn ≈ $105K/month (2,100,000 / 20 = 105,000).
- Also: is the $72K/month net burn (cash outflow after revenue) or gross burn (total cash outflow before revenue)? And are all amounts USD?

If you confirm which interpretation is correct I’ll tailor the memo. Meanwhile, below is a ready-to-use board summary + key risks and mitigations using both interpretations so you can pick the one that fits.

Board Summary (executive lead)
- Headline: Metro Manufacturing finished last quarter with revenue of $1.8M and a cash balance of $2.1M. Depending on the burn definition, runway is either roughly 29 months (if net burn = $72K/mo) or ~20 months (if net burn ≈ $105K/mo). We are generating meaningful top-line but must decide whether to preserve optionality (raise now) or prioritize operational tightening.
- Current KPIs
  - Cash on hand: $2.1M
  - Revenue (last quarter): $1.8M → ~ $600K/month
  - Reported burn: $72K/month (clarify net vs gross)
  - Runway: either ~29 months (2.1M / 72K) or ~20 months (if net burn is 105K)
- Near-term priorities (next 90 days)
  - Reconcile actual monthly net cash flow and accounts (clarify burn definition, working capital needs, AR collectability)
  - Produce a 12-month cash flow forecast under three scenarios: Base, Downside (-20% revenue), Upside (+20% revenue)
  - Decide board preference for capital strategy: no raise / small bridge / formal fundraise (timing & target)
- Recommendation (short)
  - If runway truly ≈29 months: maintain growth investments but tighten forecast discipline and monitor collections and customer concentration.
  - If runway ≈20 months: accelerate liquidity planning — refine 12-month forecast and prepare a fundraising plan or cost-reduction contingencies.

