Below is a compact, board-ready financial summary, a set of key risks with mitigations, recommended asks/decisions, and a few quick questions so I can tailor this to the board deck or a one‑page memo.

1) Quick executive summary (2–3 sentences)
- Metro Manufacturing has $2.1M cash on hand. Last quarter revenue was $1.8M, and reported burn is $72K/month — however those numbers look inconsistent (see note below). Assuming the reported burn, the company has substantial runway; focus for the board should be on working-capital management, margin improvement, and near-term liquidity contingency planning.

2) Note on numbers / discrepancy you should confirm
- With $2.1M cash and a burn of $72K/month, implied runway = 2,100,000 / 72,000 ≈ 29.2 months.
- You stated runway = 20 months; that implies an effective net cash burn of 2,100,000 / 20 = $105K/month.
- Also: last quarter revenue $1.8M → ~ $600K/month. If that is cash-collected revenue (and gross margin is positive), net cash burn should be far lower than $72K — possibly negative (cash accreting). This suggests:
  - “Burn” may be defined differently (e.g., operating cash flow after CAPEX, debt service, one-off items), or
  - There are large working capital needs (AR not collected), debt service or upcoming liabilities, or
  - The runway number is a conservative plan that builds in contingencies.
- Ask: confirm whether burn = net cash burn after revenue and debt service, and whether the $1.8M is cash or recognized/ACCRUED revenue.

3) One‑page board summary (ready text)
- Title: Financial Update — Metro Manufacturing (Date)
- Financial snapshot
  - Cash: $2.1M
  - Reported runway: 20 months (please confirm calculation)
  - Reported net cash burn: $72K/month (please confirm whether this includes debt service, capex, working capital)
  - Revenue (last quarter): $1.8M ($600K/month)
- Key headline
  - Revenue stayed at $1.8M this past quarter; cash balance is $2.1M. Primary focus: preserve liquidity and accelerate margin improvements while resolving any working-capital shortfalls that affect cash collections.
- KPIs to show (most recent and trend vs prior quarter)
  - Monthly revenue ($)
  - Gross margin (%)
  - EBITDA / Net cash burn ($/month)
  - DSO (days sales outstanding) and DPO (days payable outstanding)
  - Inventory days
  - Cash runway (base / downside / best case)
- Actions taken this quarter (sample)
  - Reduced discretionary spend by X%
  - Negotiated extended payment terms with supplier Y
  - Initiated review of low-margin SKUs
- Issues & decisions requested of the board (high level)
  - Approve contingency bridge financing or an equity raise target of $X (if needed)
  - Approve hiring freeze / discretionary spend cap
  - Approve prioritized capex deferrals of $Y
- Bottom line / ask
  - Management requests board approval to (select one or more): a short bridge facility up to $X; authority to implement headcount and capex cuts; and support for customer collection escalation.

4) Key risks (prioritized) with suggested mitigations
- A. Cash/Working-capital risk (High)
  - Why: Manufacturing often requires inventory and has receivables. If AR increases or inventory grows, cash can be consumed quickly.
  - Mitigations: tighten credit terms, escalate collections, offer early-pay discounts to strategic customers, convert slow-moving inventory to sale/offload, negotiate extended supplier terms.
  - Metric to watch: DSO, inventory days, weekly cash burn.
- B. Revenue concentration or slowdown (High)
  - Why: Dependence on a few large customers or cyclical demand could sharply reduce revenue.
  - Mitigations: diversify customer base, accelerate sales pipeline, add short-term promotions with targeted margin protections, re-prioritize high-margin product lines.
  - Metric: % revenue from top 5 customers, pipeline conversion rate.
- C. Margin compression / input-cost inflation (Medium–High)
  - Why: Raw material or freight cost increases can compress margins.
  - Mitigations: pass-through pricing where possible, hedge critical inputs, renegotiate supplier contracts, reduce low-margin SKUs.
  - Metric: gross margin vs plan, cost per unit trends.
- D. Debt covenants / financing risk (Medium)
  - Why: Covenants or upcoming maturities could require cash or restrict operations.
  - Mitigations: engage lenders early, request covenant waivers, prepare contingency financing plan.
  - Metric: covenant headroom, maturities in next 12 months.
- E. Operational / supply chain disruptions (Medium)
  - Why: Single-source suppliers, lead-time volatility, or factory downtime.
  - Mitigations: dual-source critical components, build safety stock for short lead items, cross-train staff.
  - Metric: supplier lead times, on-time delivery, production utilization.
- F. Execution risk on cost reductions (Medium)
  - Why: Rapid cuts can harm growth or key capabilities.
  - Mitigations: prioritize low-impact cost savings first; maintain minimal critical hires; define KPIs for cuts.
  - Metric: cost savings realized vs target and impact on revenue.
- G. Regulatory/compliance or product quality issues (Low–Medium)
  - Mitigations: maintain QA checks, escalate compliance reviews.

5) Scenario analysis (cash runway under three scenarios)
- Inputs to finalize: current cash ($2.1M), net monthly cash burn (confirm whether $72K or $105K), planned changes (savings, revenue growth).
- If net burn = $72K/month → runway ≈ 29 months.
- If net burn = $105K/month (implied by 20-month runway) → runway = 20 months.
- Downside example: if revenue declines 20% and burn rises to $150K/month → runway ≈ 14 months.
- Upside example: cost cuts reduce burn to $30K/month → runway ≈ 70 months (unlikely without revenue changes).
- Recommendation: present base / downside / best-case runway to the board with clear triggers for when to raise external capital.

6) Recommended immediate board items (concrete)
- Confirm and present reconciled cash-flow model showing how runway is calculated (include working capital schedule).
- Approve a 90-day liquidity contingency plan: (a) target bridge financing amount (e.g., cover 6 months of downside cash burn), (b) hiring & discretionary spend freeze authority, (c) top 5 supply/cusomer mitigation actions.
- Set financial covenants monitoring cadence (weekly cash, monthly DSO, monthly gross margin).
- Ask the board to authorize management to negotiate an accordion credit line and present term sheet options by next board meeting.

7) Suggested talking points for the CEO at the board meeting
- “We have $2.1M in cash and delivered $1.8M revenue last quarter. We are currently monitoring working capital closely because our cash runway depends on collections and inventory turns.”
- “Our highest near-term risk is cash tied up in AR and inventory; we recommend board approval for a contingency line and prioritized collection actions.”
- “We will present a reconciled cash-flow model and three scenarios at the end of the month to define exact funding needs.”

8) Next steps I can do for you (pick any)
- Turn this summary into a one‑page PDF or slide for the board.
- Draft a short board email with the summary and attach the cash model (you provide the model or I can draft a template).
- Prepare a 3–5 slide deck for the board meeting (financials, scenarios, asks, risks).
- If you want, I can draft the actual board memo or an email to circulate.

Questions for you so I can finalize the material
1. Which runway number should I use as authoritative (20 months per you, or calculated 29.2 months from $72K burn)? Or supply the exact burn definition (gross vs net, includes debt service?).
2. Is the $1.8M last-quarter revenue cash collected or recognized revenue (and are there material receivables outstanding)?
3. Any debt, covenants, or upcoming maturities in the next 12 months I should include (amounts & dates)?
4. Do you have target asks (e.g., authorize up to $X bridge financing or equity raise target)?
5. Do you want a one-pager, a slide deck, or a draft email to send to the board? If email/slide, who is the recipient list?

If you confirm the numbers or answer the questions above, I’ll prepare the board one-pager and a suggested email to send (I can draft it without sending, or draft-and-send if you instruct me to send and give recipients).

