2) Finance narrative for the board (3 short paragraphs you can drop into the deck)
Paragraph 1 — Current position
- As of the most recent close Metro holds $2.1M in cash. Revenue last quarter was $1.8M, demonstrating strong top-line performance for the period. Management’s reported runway is 20 months; we are validating that runway assumption against net cash burn and receivables timing to ensure the outlook is conservative and cash-driven.

Paragraph 2 — Underlying cash dynamics
- On the cash flow side our reported monthly burn is $72K. If that represents net cash burn after operating receipts, our liquidity position supports well over a year of runway (roughly 29 months), giving the company time to invest in prioritized growth initiatives or to raise capital on favorable terms if desired. If $72K is a gross expense figure (and not net of revenue), or if there are significant near-term cash outflows not reflected in that figure, runway and funding needs will differ materially.

Paragraph 3 — Near-term focus and asks
- Over the next 3–6 months we recommend prioritizing collection of receivables, validating assumptions behind the 20-month runway, slowing any discretionary spend until cash assumptions are reconciled, and preparing one of two scenarios for the board: (A) base-case where current burn and revenue trends hold and no raise is required in the next 12–18 months, and (B) downside case assuming lower cash collections or investments that reduce runway to <12 months. We will present scenario P&Ls and cash-flow projections at the next board meeting.

3) Key risks and recommended mitigations
- Cash-collection / AR risk: If a material portion of the $1.8M revenue is not yet collected, cash inflows could be lower than assumed.
  - Mitigation: Prioritize AR collections, enforce payment terms, offer short-term discounts for early payment, and run daily/weekly cash forecasting.

- Customer concentration: If revenue is concentrated in a few large customers, loss or delayed payment from one could quickly change cash projections.
  - Mitigation: Monitor top-10 customers, diversify sales pipeline, and secure partial prepayments or milestone billing where possible.

- Forecast assumptions / seasonality: If runway used optimistic revenue growth or delayed capex/hire timing, actual runway could be shorter.
  - Mitigation: Provide the board with a base, upside, and downside 12–18 month cash model and stress-test scenarios (e.g., revenue -20%, AR days +30).

- Unexpected capex or one-time spend: Planned investments (new line, tooling, certifications) can quickly erode cash.
  - Mitigation: Create an approval threshold for capex and require board sign-off for >$X spend; prioritize projects by ROI/time-to-payback.

- Supply chain or inflationary pressure on margins: Rising input costs could reduce gross margins and increase net burn.
  - Mitigation: Lock in supplier contracts, pass through costs where market allows, and improve yield/efficiency.

- Fundraising timing / market window: If a raise is required, market conditions can lengthen time-to-close and require bridging capital.
  - Mitigation: Prepare a fundraising deck and investor list now; define target raise size and acceptable valuation ranges; consider a small bridge facility if runway <12 months.

4) Suggested board asks / decisions
- Confirm runway calculation method and approve a timeline to finalize the cash model (deadline: e.g., 2 weeks).
- Approve temporary hiring/capex freeze for discretionary items until cash model is reconciled.
- Authorize management to pursue improvement actions (AR collection program, negotiating supplier terms).
- Approve engagement with 2–3 potential lenders/investors for a precautionary line if downside scenario shows <9–12 months runway.

5) KPIs and slides to include in the board pack (must-have)
- Cash balance (current) and projected monthly balance for 12–18 months (base/upside/downside)
- Net cash burn (monthly) and how it’s calculated (explicit formula)
- Revenue run-rate and AR days / cash collected vs billed last quarter
- Gross margin and change vs prior quarter
- Customer concentration (Top 5 customers % of revenue)
- Capex / committed spend and planned hires
- Funding plan / trigger points (e.g., pursue raise if runway <12 months)

6) Example short CEO/CFO narrative (2–3 sentences) you can read aloud
- “We finished the quarter with $2.1M in cash and strong revenue of $1.8M. Our current reported runway is 20 months; we are reconciling this with net cash burn and receivables timing and will present a finalized cash outlook with base and downside scenarios at the next board meeting. In the meantime we recommend prioritizing AR collections and pausing discretionary capex until the forecast is confirmed.”

