Board summary (executive paragraph)
- Executive summary: Metro Manufacturing currently shows $2.1M in cash, last quarter revenue of $1.8M (≈ $600K/month average), and a reported runway of 20 months (please confirm). Operating cash burn is stated as $72K/month. Overall performance shows healthy revenue relative to burn, but we must validate working capital, customer concentration, and committed cash outflows to confirm runway. Near-term priorities are (1) confirm cash runway and assumptions, (2) secure contingency liquidity or cost flexibility to protect against demand or supply shocks, and (3) tighten AR/collections and inventory to preserve cash.

Key financial snapshot (for a slide)
- Cash: $2.1M
- Last quarter revenue: $1.8M (≈ $600K/month)
- Operating burn: $72K/month (please confirm)
- Implied runway at $72K burn: ~29 months (note: user stated 20 months — please confirm)
- Revenue-to-burn ratio: revenue (~$600K/mo) is ~8.3x monthly burn (strong buffer on a monthly basis), but seasonality, lumpy collections, or capex commitments can reduce effective runway
- Immediate ask of Board: confirm acceptance of updated forecast and approve contingency financing authority (if needed)

Top-priority risks (with impact, likelihood, early warning indicators, and mitigations)
1) Liquidity shock or hidden cash commitments
 - Impact: High (could force emergency financing or rapid cost cuts)
 - Likelihood: Medium (depends on undisclosed commitments/one-time outflows)
 - Early indicators: large vendor invoices due, sudden capex orders, draws on lines, or negative working capital shifts
 - Mitigations: confirm a 13-week cashflow, freeze non-essential capex, negotiate vendor terms, and prepare a bridge financing term sheet

2) Revenue slowdown / customer concentration
 - Impact: High (if top customers represent large share)
 - Likelihood: Medium
 - Early indicators: slowdown in orders, lengthening AR days, single-customer payment delays
 - Mitigations: diversify customers, accelerate collections (discounts for early pay), tighten credit terms, prioritize high-margin orders

3) Supply chain disruption raising costs or delaying production
 - Impact: Medium–High
 - Likelihood: Medium
 - Early indicators: supplier delivery slippage, cost increases quoted, inventory shortages
 - Mitigations: identify alternate suppliers, negotiate fixed-price short-term contracts, maintain safety stock for critical parts

4) Forecast / budgeting errors and optimistic assumptions
 - Impact: Medium
 - Likelihood: Medium–High (common)
 - Early indicators: variances vs forecast, recurring one-off adjustments
 - Mitigations: run sensitivity scenarios, reforecast monthly, require variance explanations >5%

5) Working capital (AR / inventory) deterioration
 - Impact: Medium
 - Likelihood: Medium
 - Early indicators: DSO increase, inventory aging
 - Mitigations: tighten credit policy, collection incentives, discount slow-moving inventory, consider short-term receivables financing if needed

6) Need for additional financing / covenant risk
 - Impact: High
 - Likelihood: Low–Medium
 - Early indicators: covenant breaches, lender inquiries
 - Mitigations: early lender communication, prepare covenant waiver requests, identify lender/Investor options

Recommended actions for management / board asks
- Near-term (0–90 days)
  - Produce a 13-week cashflow and updated monthly forecast (showing best/base/worst cases).
  - Validate the runway calculation and list committed cash outflows.
  - Freeze discretionary spend and postpone non-essential hiring/capex.
  - Tighten AR collections — assign an owner and weekly targets.
  - Prepare a contingency financing plan (term sheet(s)) and a prioritized list of cuts if needed.
- Medium-term (90–180 days)
  - Improve customer diversification and sales pipeline visibility.
  - Optimize inventory turns and supplier contracts.
  - Present monthly KPI pack to board: cash, runway, DSO, inventory days, gross margin, and actual vs forecast.

Suggested board narrative / talking points (3–4 minutes)
- “We entered the quarter with $2.1M of cash and reported runway of [confirm]. Revenue last quarter was $1.8M. On a monthly basis our revenue is roughly $600K vs burn of $72K, which gives us a strong revenue-to-burn cushion. That said, we have identified a couple of exposures — primarily potential committed cash outflows and customer concentration — which could reduce our effective runway. We’ve prepared a 13-week cash plan and are proposing a temporary capex freeze, tightened AR collections, and a contingency financing plan to deploy if needed. With board approval of these moves and authority to pursue a short-term credit facility if required, we expect to preserve optionality while continuing to invest in high-return growth items.”
 - Close: “We’ll circulate the detailed forecast and stress scenarios for your review. We request the board’s approval to execute contingency financing discussions and to implement the temporary operating controls outlined.”

Suggested slide deck outline (6–8 slides)
1) Title & ask (1 slide) — One-line summary and decisions requested
2) Current position (1 slide) — cash, revenue, burn, stated runway, working capital snapshot
3) Key drivers & recent trend (1 slide) — revenue trend, margin, major customers
4) 13-week cash forecast & scenarios (1 slide) — base, downside, stress
5) Key risks & mitigations (1 slide) — prioritized as above
6) Proposed actions & timeline (1 slide) — near-term and medium-term asks
7) Contingency financing plan (1 slide) — options, approximate terms, next steps
8) Appendix (sensitivity tables, detailed forecast, KPIs)

Quick scenario examples (useful for board Q&A)
- Base: burn = $72K → implied runway ≈ 29 months. Minimal financing need; focus on working capital.
- Downside: burn +25% (to $90K) or revenue down 20% → runway 2.1M/90K ≈ 23 months (still >1 year, but limits flexibility).
- Stress: one major customer delayed payments or one-time cash obligation of $600K → runway materially reduced; demonstrates need for a 13-week plan and potential short-term liquidity.

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft a 1–page board memo using the confirmed runway number and any additional assumptions you want included.
- Create slide deck content (I can produce slide text for each slide above).
- Draft an email to the board or finance committee with the materials attached (I will NOT send to third parties unless you explicitly ask me to send and provide recipients).

