- Tone: factual, transparent, and forward-looking; call out assumptions and specific asks.

Priority asks / decisions for the board
- Approve revised 90-day cash plan and contingency thresholds (e.g., when to pause hiring or defer projects).
- Authorize short-term working capital actions: tighten AR terms, prioritize collections, and potentially offer early-pay discounts to strategic customers.
- Approve target metrics and reporting cadence: weekly cash update, monthly KPI pack (revenue by customer, AR days, gross margin, backlog).
- If appropriate, authorize engagement with investors or lenders to maintain optionality.

Key risks (ranked, with mitigation and monitoring metric)

1) Revenue concentration / customer risk
- Why it matters: Large customers or a small number of accounts can materially reduce revenue quickly.
- Mitigation: Diversify pipeline, negotiate multi-quarter commitments, and push for contractual protections (minimum purchase or notice periods).
- Metrics to monitor: % revenue from top 5 customers, customer churn rate, pipeline by stage.

2) Cash conversion (AR / collections)
- Why it matters: High receivables delay cash inflows even if booked revenue looks strong.
- Mitigation: Tighten payment terms, offer early-payment incentives, strengthen credit checks, prioritize collections team resources on major accounts.
- Metrics: Days Sales Outstanding (DSO), aging buckets, % of AR > 60/90 days.

3) Supply chain / production disruptions
- Why it matters: Delays or input-cost spikes can impact margin and delivery schedules, causing cancellations or penalty exposure.
- Mitigation: Secure alternate suppliers, maintain safety stock for critical items, renegotiate lead times or price clauses.
- Metrics: Supplier lead-time variance, inventory days, on-time delivery rate.

4) Margin compression / rising input costs
- Why it matters: If revenue growth is accompanied by lower gross margin, cash runway shrinks faster.
- Mitigation: Focus sales on highest-margin products, pass through costs where contractually possible, improve yield/efficiency.
- Metrics: Gross margin %, product-level margin, cost per unit trends.

5) Unexpected cost increases / staffing costs
- Why it matters: Headcount or one-off costs can materially increase burn.
- Mitigation: Freeze non-critical hires, institute hiring approvals, and build a prioritized capex and hiring plan.
- Metrics: Monthly operating spend, hiring pipeline, headcount by function.

6) Financing / covenant risk (if any debt)
- Why it matters: Debt covenants or credit lines can restrict options and trigger costly remedies.
- Mitigation: Review covenants, hold lender conversations early, maintain covenant headroom.
- Metrics: Covenant headroom, unused credit lines, upcoming maturities.

7) Macro demand shock / customer insolvency
- Why it matters: Broader economic slowdown can reduce orders and increase receivables risk.
- Mitigation: Stress-test scenarios, maintain hiring/cost flexibility, and accelerate business development in more resilient segments.
- Metrics: Order backlog, RFP wins, month-over-month revenue trend.

